    ],
    "bottomLine": "The easiest way to clear a front walk and a couple of steps with one plug, and it expands with more HeatTrak mats.",
    "description": "Most slips happen on the last few feet: the steps up to the door and the short walk before them. HeatTrak's Starter Kit covers exactly that. The listing includes one 20 by 60 inch walkway mat, two 10 by 30 inch stair mats, the power unit and one cable extender, so you can lay the walkway mat at the bottom, run the extender up the stairs and clear the top two treads from a single outlet. The spec that makes planning easy is printed on the listing: each 10 by 30 inch stair tread draws 0.7 amps. That lets you work out how many more treads you can add before you reach the power unit's limit, once you have the walkway mat's own draw from its label or HeatTrak's spec sheet. HeatTrak lists the same 2 inches per hour melt rate as its other mats, describes the material as corrosion-resistant and built to be left out all season, and backs the kit with a 2-year warranty. The trade-off is coverage: two stair mats suit a short stoop, not a full flight of six or eight steps, so most buyers end up ordering extra stair mats. If you only have a flat walk, the single walkway mat with power unit is cheaper. If your steps are wider than 30 inches, look at HeatTrak's longer 10 by 48 inch stair mats instead."
  },
  {
    "id": "heattrak-entrance-mat-40x60",
    "name": "HeatTrak Heated Entrance Mat with GFCI Power Cord (40 x 60 in)",
    "category": "40 x 60 in Entrance Mat, Power Unit with 6 ft Cord, 2 in/hr",
    "badge": "Best for Entrances",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Large 40 x 60 in mat covers a full landing or garage door",
      "Title lists a GFCI power cord; power unit has a 6 ft cord",
      "Power unit can also run added stair and walkway mats",
      "Listed melt rate of 2 inches per hour",
      "2-year manufacturer's warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Big and heavy to roll up and store",
      "Amp draw for this size is not on the listing",
      "6 ft cord means the outlet must be close"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick for a wide landing, porch or side door where a narrow walkway mat leaves icy edges.",
    "description": "A 20 inch walkway mat clears a path, but a front landing, a porch in front of a double door or the apron of a side garage door needs width. HeatTrak's 40 by 60 inch entrance mat covers a landing roughly three feet by five, enough for two people to stand on while they find their keys. The listing title says it includes a GFCI power cord, and the features describe an outdoor power unit with a 6 foot cord that can also run stair and walkway mats, so you can start with the landing and add treads below it. HeatTrak lists the same melt rate of 2 inches per hour and a corrosion-resistant build meant to stay outside all season, with a 2-year warranty. Two things to plan for. First, the listing does not state this mat's amp draw, and a mat this size draws far more than a stair tread, so read the label before chaining anything to it; this listing gives the power unit's limit as 14 amps, while others in the range say 13. Second, the 6 foot cord means the mat must sit near an outdoor GFCI outlet; use only an outdoor-rated cord if HeatTrak's instructions allow one. If your landing is smaller than about three feet square, the Cozy Products Ice-Away or a single walkway mat is enough."
  },
  {
    "id": "toriexon-20x60-330w",
    "name": "Toriexon 20 x 60 in Heated Snow Melting Mat (330W, 120V)",
    "category": "20 x 60 in Walkway Mat, 330W, 120V, 2 in/hr, UL/ETL Cord (Listed)",
    "badge": "Best Value Walkway Mat",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Wattage printed on the listing: 330W at 120V",
      "Listed melt rate of up to 2 inches per hour",
      "UL and ETL certified power cord and plug, per the listing",
      "Anti-slip, waterproof surface",
      "Clear chaining limit: keep the total under 1,500W"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No GFCI built into the plug; it must go in a GFCI outlet",
      "No low-temperature rating given",
      "Thin listing: no warranty terms stated"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A 5-foot walkway mat with honest power numbers on the listing, for less than the HeatTrak.",
    "description": "Toriexon's 20 by 60 inch mat is the same footprint as HeatTrak's walkway mat and suits a buyer who wants to clear a 5-foot stretch of path for less. What sets this listing apart is that it gives the power numbers plainly: 330 watts at 120 volts, which works out to roughly 2.75 amps by our arithmetic. Toriexon also gives a chaining rule, telling buyers to keep the total power of connected mats at or under 1,500 watts. That means up to four of these mats on one run (1,320 watts), provided the circuit has nothing else on it. The listing describes a melt rate of up to 2 inches of snow per hour, an anti-slip waterproof surface, and a UL and ETL certified power cord and plug designed for outdoor use. It tells you to plug into an outdoor GFCI outlet, and you should take that literally: there is no GFCI listed on the plug itself. The listing calls these heated driveway mats, but it gives no weight or vehicle rating, so treat it as a walkway mat unless Toriexon confirms otherwise. The trade-offs are thin details: there is no lowest working temperature and no warranty term stated, so ask the seller before you buy. If you want a system with a known warranty and power unit, pay more for the HeatTrak."
  },
  {
    "id": "easeroll-24x144",
    "name": "EaseRoll 24 x 144 in Heated Snow Melting Mat (900W)",
    "category": "24 in x 12 ft Runner, 900W, 7.5A, 120V, 10 ft Cord, TPU Surface",
    "badge": "Best Long Runner for Driveway Tracks",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "12 ft long and 24 in wide: 24 sq ft from one plug",
      "Full electrical specs listed: 120V, 900W, 7.5A",
      "TPU surface designed for temperatures down to -22°F",
      "Rolls up for storage once it has cooled",
      "Title lists a melt rate of 4 inches per hour"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Only one mat this size per connected setup",
      "Two tire tracks need two mats on separate circuits",
      "No vehicle weight rating on the listing"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The longest single plug-in run here, for a long walk or one lane of a short driveway, used on its own circuit.",
    "description": "A 12 foot runner is the closest thing on Amazon to a plug-in heated driveway mat, and EaseRoll's listing is more complete than most. It gives 120 volts, 900 watts and 7.5 amps with a 10 foot power cord, a 24 by 144 inch size covering 24 square feet, a thickened TPU surface designed for temperatures down to -22 degrees Fahrenheit, and graphene heating that reaches a 140 degree Fahrenheit setting. The title claims a melt rate of 4 inches per hour, while the text describes melting snow in covered areas, so take the headline number as a best case. The rule that shapes how you use it: EaseRoll says to run only one mat of this size per connected setup, with a maximum system current of 13 amps. At 7.5 amps, one mat already uses most of a standard 15 amp circuit's safe continuous load, so two mats for two tire tracks need two separate circuits, ideally two GFCI outlets on different breakers. EaseRoll also says to unroll it fully, never cover it, insulate it on wood surfaces, let it cool before rolling and switch it off when the snow is gone. There is no vehicle weight rating, so check with the seller before you drive on it. For a whole driveway, compare the installed systems on our heated driveway cost page instead."
  },
  {
    "id": "hiraliy-3pc-silicone-10x30",
    "name": "HIRALIY 3-Pack Silicone Snow Melting Mats (10 x 30 in)",
    "category": "3 x 10 x 30 in Stair Mats, Built-In GFCI Plug, IP67, Up to 3 in/hr",
    "badge": "Best Budget Stair Set",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Built-in GFCI plug with TEST and RESET buttons",
      "IP67 rating with threaded caps on each connector",
      "Silicone surface helps stop melt from refreezing",
      "Three stair-sized mats in one pack",
      "Listed melt rate of up to 3 inches per hour"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Wattage and amp draw are not on the listing",
      "No lowest operating temperature given",
      "Three mats cover only three steps"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A cheaper way to cover three steps, with a GFCI built into the plug and sealed connectors.",
    "description": "Stairs are where a heated mat earns its keep, and the HIRALIY 3-pack is a lower-cost way to cover three of them. Each mat is the standard 10 by 30 inch stair size, and the mats connect to one another and to a single plug. The feature that matters most is safety: the listing describes a built-in GFCI plug with TEST and RESET buttons and red indicator lights, so you can check the protection works before the first storm. You should still use a GFCI outdoor outlet, but the extra protection at the plug is welcome on a product that sits in slush all winter. The mats are rated IP67, and each connector has a threaded protective cap that the listing says to tighten fully after connecting; loose caps are the most likely way water gets in. HIRALIY lists a silicone surface with raised texture that helps stop melted snow from refreezing, a surface temperature of up to roughly 140 degrees Fahrenheit and a melt rate of up to 3 inches per hour. The listing does not give watts or amps, so read the label before adding mats or sharing the circuit. If your staircase has more than three steps, or you want a known amp figure per tread, HeatTrak's 10 by 30 inch stair mats at 0.7 amps each make planning easier."
  },
  {
    "id": "cozy-products-ice-away",
    "name": "Cozy Products Ice-Away Snow Melting Mat",
    "category": "Approx. 38 x 34 in Doorstep Mat, 240W, 110V, 15 ft Cord, Butyl Rubber",
    "badge": "Best Single Doorstep Mat",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Low 240W draw, about 2 amps by our arithmetic",
      "Long 15 ft power cord reaches a distant outlet",
      "UV-stabilized butyl rubber with a non-slip surface",
      "Melts about 2 inches of new snow in under an hour, per Cozy",
      "Simple single mat: no chaining or power unit"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Listing mentions both TUV and ETL listing; check the label",
      "Does not connect to other mats",
