        <div id="how-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Do Snow Melting Mats Work? Heat From Below at About 2 Inches an Hour
          </h2>

          <p>
            A snow melting mat is a rubber, PVC, TPU or silicone mat with a heating element sealed inside. You lay it on a walkway, step or landing, plug it into an outdoor outlet, and it warms the surface enough to melt snow as it lands. The best snow melting mat is the one sized for your path, with a melt rate and amp draw you can actually plan around.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Heating Element</h3>

          <p>
            Older mats use resistance wire; many newer Amazon listings describe graphene or carbon heating film. The material matters less than the wattage and how evenly the heat covers the mat. Most listings give a heating area slightly smaller than the mat itself, so the edges stay cooler. Walensee, for example, lists a 16.1 by 55.5 inch heating area on a 20 by 60 inch mat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Melt Rate: Read It as Snowfall, Not a Pile</h3>

          <p>
            Makers list melt rates of 2 to 4 inches per hour. HeatTrak lists 2 inches per hour across its range; EaseRoll's title claims 4. These figures describe keeping up with snow as it falls. A mat switched on under a foot of drifted snow will melt a pocket underneath and leave a crust on top, so turn mats on before the storm, not after it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do They Work in Deep Cold?</h3>

          <p>
            A mat heats its surface relative to the air. HeatTrak says its surface reaches about 40 degrees Fahrenheit above the ambient temperature and works well down to -8 degrees Fahrenheit. Below that, the surface may hover near freezing and clearing slows. Wind and an uninsulated surface such as a raised wooden deck also pull heat away. In the coldest regions, expect a mat to keep a path passable rather than bare and dry.
          </p>
        </div>

